a, NJ</publisher-loc></citation></ref><ref id="Tarranetal2001a"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Tarran</surname><given-names>R.</given-names></name><name><surname>Grubb</surname><given-names>B.R.</given-names></name><name><surname>Gatzy</surname><given-names>J.T.</given-names></name><name><surname>Davis</surname><given-names>C.W.</given-names></name><name><surname>Boucher</surname><given-names>R.C.</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>The relative roles of passive surface forces and active ion transport in the modulation of airway surface liquid volume and composition</article-title><source>J. Gen. Physiol.</source><volume>118</volume><year>2001</year><fpage>223</fpage><lpage>236</lpage>a<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11479349</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="Tarranetal2001b"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Tarran</surname><given-names>R.</given-names></name><name><surname>Grubb</surname><given-names>B.R.</given-names></name><name><surname>Parsons</surname><given-names>D.</given-names></name><name><surname>Picher</surname><given-names>M.</given-names></name><name><surname>Hirsch</surname><given-names>A.J.</given-names></name><name><surname>Davis</surname><given-names>C.W.</given-names></name><name><surname>Boucher</surname><given-names>R.C.</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>The CF salt controversyIn vivo observations and therapeutic approaches</article-title><source>Mol. Cell.</source><volume>8</volume><year>2001</year><fpage>149</fpage><lpage>158</lpage>b<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11511368</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="Travisetal1999"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Travis</surname><given-names>S.M.</given-names></name><name><surname>Conway</surname><given-names>B.A.</given-names></name><name><surname>Zabner</surname><given-names>J.</given-names></name><name><surname>Smith</surname><given-names>J.J.</given-names></name><name><surname>Anderson</surname><given-names>N.N.</given-names></name><name><surname>Singh</surname><given-names>P.K.</given-names></name><name><surname>Greenberg</surname><given-names>E.P.</given-names></name><name><surname>Welsh</surname><given-names>M.J.</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Activity of abundant antimicrobials of the human airway</article-title><source>Am. J. Respir. Cell Mol. Biol.</source><volume>20</volume><year>1999</year><fpage>872</fpage><lpage>879</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">10226057</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="Verkman2001"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Verkman</surname><given-names>A.S.</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Lung disease in cystic fibrosisis airway surface liquid composition abnormal?</article-title><source>Am. J. Physiol. Lung Cell Mol. Physiol.</source><volume>281</volume><year>2001</year><fpage>L306</fpage><lpage>L308</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11435202</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="Widdicombe1999"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Widdicombe</surname><given-names>J.H.</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Altered NaCl concentration of airway surface liquid in cystic fibrosis</article-title><source>News Physiol. Sci.</source><volume>14</volume><year>1999</year><fpage>126</fpage><lpage>127</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11390837</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="Wine1999"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Wine</surname><given-names>J.J.</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>The genesis of cystic fibrosis lung disease</article-title><source>J. Clin. Invest.</source><volume>103</volume><year>1999</year><fpage>309</fpage><lpage>312</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">9927490</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="Wolfordetal1986"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Wolford</surname><given-names>S.T.</given-names></name><name><surname>Schroer</surname><given-names>R.A.</given-names></name><name><surname>Gohs</surname><given-names>F.X.</given-names></name><name><surname>Gallo</surname><given-names>P.P.</given-names></name><name><surname>Brodeck</surname><given-names>M.</given-names></name><name><surname>Falk</surname><given-names>H.B.</given-names></name><name><surname>Ruhren</surname><given-names>R.</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Reference range data base for serum chemistry and hematology values in laboratory animals</article-title><source>J. Toxicol. Environ. Health.</source><volume>18</volume><year>1986</year><fpage>161</fpage><lpage>188</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">3712484</pub-id></citation></ref></ref-list><fn-group><fn><p>The online version of this article contains supplemental material.</p></fn><fn><p><italic>Abbreviations used in this paper:</italic> ASL, airway surface liquid&#x0003b; CF, cystic fibrosis&#x0003b; LR, lactated Ringer's solution.</p></fn></fn-group></back><floats-wrap><fig id="F1"><label>Figure 1</label><caption><p>Cl<sup>&#x02212;</sup>-electrode sensor used for ASL Cl<sup>&#x02212;</sup> measurements. (A) Cross-sectional view of the Ag/AgCl sensor and the air&#x02013;surface liquid profile above airway epithelium is illustrated. ASL [Cl<sup>&#x02212;</sup>] is obtained from the electrode voltage (<xref ref-type="disp-formula" rid="FD1"/>) developed from the electrochemistry at the electrode&#x02013;electrolyte interface. The low impedance of the interface does not require the electrode to be totally immersed for Cl<sup>&#x02212;</sup> measurement. The illustrated air&#x02013;liquid radius of curvature is from confocal imaging (see below). The illustrated surface liquid height at the edge of the epithelium is from mouse trachea (<xref ref-type="bib" rid="Jayaramanetal2001a">Jayaraman et al. 2001a</xref>). For clarity, the PE-50 catheter containing the reference electrode is not shown. (B) Confocal image of the surface liquid&#x02013;electrode interface. The electrode-induced radius of curvature was 373 &#x003bc;m.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="JGP8510.f1a"/><graphic xlink:href="JGP8510.f1b"/></fig><fig id="F2"><label>Figure 2</label><caption><p>Representative electrode recording and analysis used to determine [Cl<sup>&#x02212;</sup>] in various salt solutions. (A) Cl<sup>&#x02212;</sup>-electrode recording in lactated Ringer's (LR) solution is shown. The electrode response was recorded in Cl<sup>&#x02212;</sup> calibration solutions (in millimolar units, labeled above the trace). The electrometer output voltage was zeroed in 10 mM Cl<sup>&#x02212;</sup> solution and switched to ground between measurements. (B) The LR [Cl<sup>&#x02212;</sup>] (113.0 mM, &#x02022;) was determined from the sample voltage response and E(mV) = 52.8 &#x000b7; log[Cl<sup>&#x02212;</sup>] &#x02212; 53.0 (<xref ref-type="disp-formula" rid="FD1"/>&#x0003b; solid line fitted to &#x025cb; calibration symbols). Open squares partially mask open circles and represent the responses obtained in the calibration standards after LR Cl<sup>&#x02212;</sup> measurement. Note log-scale for abscissa. Electrode drift was 0.1 mV/min (r<sup>2</sup> = 0.988). For each salt solution, at least three measurements were made in &#x0223c;5-ml solution volumes (23&#x02013;25&#x000b0;C).</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="JGP8510.f2a"/><graphic xlink:href="JGP8510.f2b"/></fig><fig position="float" id="F3"><label>Figure 3</label><caption><p>ASL [Cl<sup>&#x02212;</sup>] is decreased after 30 mM Cl<sup>&#x02212;</sup> instillation. Tracheal Cl<sup>&#x02212;</sup>-electrode recording is indicated (T) after the calibration procedure (see <xref ref-type="fig" rid="F2">Fig. 2</xref> legend). The ASL [Cl<sup>&#x02212;</sup>] decreased from 121.6 to 65.0 mM after instilling 55 &#x003bc;l (37&#x000b0;C) of the low Cl<sup>&#x02212;</sup> solution (S). The ASL Cl<sup>&#x02212;</sup> concentrations were obtained from E(mV) = 57.4 &#x000b7; log[Cl<sup>&#x02212;</sup>] &#x02212; 81.2. Subsequently, the electrode voltage recovered. The recovery was well described by E(mV) = 23.3 + 15.7(1 &#x02212; e<sup>&#x02212;t/47.5s</sup>) (solid curve, adjusted r<sup>2</sup> = 0.968). Unfilled circles represent data points collected at 5-s intervals to illustrate clearly the fitted kinetic model.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="JGP8510.f3"/></fig><fig position="float" id="F4"><label>Figure 4</label><caption><p>Right main bronchus (RMB) Cl<sup>&#x02212;</sup>-electrode recording in rabbit. After electrode calibration (0&#x02013;5 min), the voltage response during the 66-s RMB recording was 34.7 &#x000b1; 0.3 mV (mean &#x000b1; SD&#x0003b; range: 34.1&#x02013;35.7 mV) corresponding to a [Cl<sup>&#x02212;</sup>] = 124.0 mM (E(mV) = 54.8 &#x000b7; log[Cl<sup>&#x02212;</sup>] &#x02212; 80.1&#x0003b; r<sup>2</sup> = 0.999). Stable responses were obtained in Cl<sup>&#x02212;</sup> standard solutions (15&#x02013;17 min) after the RMB measurement.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="JGP8510.f4"/></fig><fig position="float" id="F5"><label>Figure 5</label><caption><p>Transepithelial potential measurement during Cl<sup>&#x02212;</sup>-electrode recording in mouse trachea. (A) Tracheal epithelial integrity was assessed during Cl<sup>&#x02212;</sup>-electrode recordings by measuring the transepithelial potential (&#x02193;). Transepithelial potential was stable and remained polarized (lumen negative) throughout the experiment. (B) Tracheal Cl<sup>&#x02212;</sup>-electrode voltage recorded simultaneously with the transepithelial potential in A. The tracheal [Cl<sup>&#x02212;</sup>] (from E(mV) = 56.6 &#x000b7; log[Cl<sup>&#x02212;</sup>] &#x02212; 83.6&#x0003b; r<sup>2</sup> = 0.999) was 120 mM. Electrode drift was 0.08 mV/min (r<sup>2</sup> = 0.94). Lumenal tracheal temperature was 31.9 &#x000b1; 0.5&#x000b0;C, (<italic>n</italic> = 9). Calibration solution temperatures were adjusted appropriately. Break in abscissa corresponds to 32 min during the mouse anesthesia and surgical procedure. Portions of the figure were suppressed between switching the calibration solutions.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="JGP8510.f5"/></fig><fig position="float" id="F6"><label>Figure 6</label><caption><p>Summary ASL Cl<sup>&#x02212;</sup> values in various species. Mouse trachea (Trch, 112.8 &#x000b1; 4.2 mM, <italic>n</italic> = 13&#x0003b; serum [Cl<sup>&#x02212;</sup>] = 120 mM) and regional ASL [Cl<sup>&#x02212;</sup>] in rabbit (Trch, 114.3 &#x000b1; 1.8 mM, <italic>n</italic> = 6&#x0003b; serum [Cl<sup>&#x02212;</sup>] = 105 mM), bronchus (Brnch&#x0003b; 126.9 &#x000b1; 1.7 mM, <italic>n</italic> = 3), and small airways (SA&#x0003b; 108.3 &#x000b1; 7.1 mM, <italic>n</italic> = 5) are shown. Regional ASL [Cl<sup>&#x02212;</sup>] in monkey (Brnch, 112.3 &#x000b1; 10.9 mM, <italic>n</italic> = 3&#x0003b; SA, 128.5 &#x000b1; 6.8 mM, <italic>n</italic> = 3&#x0003b; serum [Cl<sup>&#x02212;</sup>] = 110 mM) also is shown. Human nasal liquid [Cl<sup>&#x02212;</sup>] after nasal occlusion (Nsl&#x0003b; 117.3 &#x000b1; 11.2 mM, <italic>n</italic> = 6&#x0003b; plasma [Cl<sup>&#x02212;</sup>] = 104 mM) is shown for comparison. In all species studied, ASL Cl<sup>&#x02212;</sup> values were comparable to the serum/plasma [Cl<sup>&#x02212;</sup>]. Serum Cl<sup>&#x02212;</sup> values are from <xref ref-type="bib" rid="Wolfordetal1986">Wolford et al. 1986</xref>.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="JGP8510.f6"/></fig></floats-wrap></article>